Here's an example of how we can represent the multi-layered neural network function in Latex:
\begin{equation}
f(X_i, Y_i, Z_i) = \begin{cases} 1, & \mbox{if } (X_i + Y_i + Z_i) > k \\ 0, & \mbox{otherwise} \end{cases}
\end{equation}
Where $X_i$, $Y_i$, and $Z_i$ are the input features for student i, and k is the threshold value that accounts for socioeconomic factors.
To train the multi-layered neural network, we can use a deep learning algorithm like CNN or RNN. Here's an example of how we can represent the optimization algorithm in Latex:
\begin{equation}
\min_{\theta} \sum_{i=1}^{n} L(f(X_i, Y_i, Z_i), y_i) + \lambda \Omega(\theta)
\end{equation}
Where $\theta$ represents the coefficients of the neural network, $L$ is the loss function, $y_i$ is the ground truth label for student i, and $\lambda$ is the regularization parameter that controls the complexity of the model. The regularization term $\Omega(\theta)$ is used to prevent overfitting and can be represented as:
\begin{equation}
\Omega(\theta) = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \sum_{i=1}^{n_l} \sum_{j=1}^{n_{l+1}} \left| \theta_{ij}^{(l)} \right|
\end{equation}
Where $L$ is the number of layers in the neural network, $n_l$ is the number of neurons in layer $l$, and $\theta_{ij}^{(l)}$ is the weight parameter connecting neuron $i$ in layer $l$ to neuron $j$ in layer $l+1$.
Overall, the implementation of this algorithm in Latex would involve defining the multi-layered neural network function and the optimization algorithm in equations, and using a deep learning library like Tensorflow or PyTorch to implement the algorithm in code.

