To determine the missing temperature values and replace them with the average of the available temperatures for that week, you can use the following shell command:
```
cat data.json | jq '.temperature_data |= map(.temperature |= if . == null then ((.[0:.-1] | map(select(.temperature != null)) | map(.temperature) | add) / (.[0:.-1] | map(select(.temperature != null)) | length)) else . end)'
```
This command uses the `jq` command-line tool to manipulate the JSON data. It first selects the `temperature_data` array using the `|= map()` operator, and then uses the `|=` operator again to modify each object in the array. For each object, it checks if the `temperature` value is null, and if so, it calculates the average of the available temperatures for that week using the `add` and `length` functions. If the `temperature` value is not null, it leaves it unchanged.
To calculate the weekly average temperature for the experiment and store it in a separate JSON object, you can use the following shell command:
```
cat data.json | jq '.temperature_data | group_by(.date[0:7]) | map({week: .[0].date[0:7], average_temperature: (map(select(.temperature != null)) | map(.temperature) | add) / (map(select(.temperature != null)) | length)})'
```
This command first selects the `temperature_data` array using the `|` operator, then groups the objects in the array by the first 7 characters of the `date` field using the `group_by()` function. It then maps each group to a new object that contains the week and the average temperature for that week, calculated using the `add` and `length` functions.
To calculate the standard deviation of the temperatures for the entire experiment and store it in the same JSON object, you can use the following shell command:
```
