def find_second_highest(numbers):
    highest = float('-inf')
    second_highest = float('-inf')
    for num in numbers:
        if num >= 0:
            if num > highest:
                second_highest = highest
                highest = num
            elif num > second_highest:
                second_highest = num
    return second_highest
```
This program first sets the highest and second highest variables to negative infinity. Then, it loops through the input list and checks if the current number is non-negative. If it is, it checks if the current number is greater than the current highest number. If it is, it sets the second highest number to the current highest number and sets the highest number to the current number. If the current number is not greater than the current highest number but is greater than the current second highest number, it sets the second highest number to the current number. Finally, it returns the second highest number.
This program is efficient because it only loops through the input list once and only performs comparisons on non-negative numbers. It can handle large input lists because it does not store the entire list in memory and only keeps track of the two highest numbers.

