To ensure that the code can handle situations where the provided coordinates do not correspond to a physical location, we can add error handling to our code. Specifically, we can use a try-catch block to catch any exceptions that may occur when we call the Google Maps API.
Here is the updated code in Scala:
```scala
import java.net.URLEncoder
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets
import scala.io.Source
import scala.util.{Failure, Success, Try}
import scala.concurrent.Future
import scala.concurrent.ExecutionContext.Implicits.global
object Geocoding {
  def getAddressFromCoordinates(lat: Double, lng: Double): Future[Option[String]] = {
    val url = s"https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=$lat,$lng&key=YOUR_API_KEY"
    val encodedUrl = URLEncoder.encode(url, StandardCharsets.UTF_8.toString)
    val response = Future {
      Try(Source.fromURL(encodedUrl).mkString)
    }
    response.map {
      case Success(jsonString) =>
        val json = Json.parse(jsonString)
        val address = (json \ "results")(0) \ "formatted_address"
        Some(address.as[String])
      case Failure(exception) =>
        println(s"Exception occurred: ${exception.getMessage}")
        None
    }
  }
}
```
In this updated code, we use a try-catch block to catch any exceptions that may occur when we call the Google Maps API. If an exception occurs, we print an error message and return None. If no exception occurs, we parse the JSON response and extract the formatted address.
Note that we are using Scala's `Future` API to make the API call asynchronous. This allows our code to continue executing while we wait for the API response.

