The corresponding letter for a substance with medium texture and thin thickness is 'A'. 
Here is the SQL query to retrieve the letter:
```
SELECT thickness FROM substance WHERE texture='Medium' AND thickness='A';
```
This query will return the letter 'A' as the result.
In C++, you could use a library such as ODBC to connect to the SQL database and execute the query. Here is an example:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <sql.h>
#include <sqlext.h>
int main() {
    SQLHENV env;
    SQLHDBC dbc;
    SQLHSTMT stmt;
    SQLRETURN ret;
    // Allocate environment handle
    ret = SQLAllocHandle(SQL_HANDLE_ENV, SQL_NULL_HANDLE, &env);
    // Set the ODBC version
    ret = SQLSetEnvAttr(env, SQL_ATTR_ODBC_VERSION, (SQLPOINTER)SQL_OV_ODBC3, 0);
    // Allocate connection handle
    ret = SQLAllocHandle(SQL_HANDLE_DBC, env, &dbc);
    // Connect to the database
    ret = SQLConnect(dbc, (SQLCHAR*)"mydsn", SQL_NTS, (SQLCHAR*)"myuser", SQL_NTS, (SQLCHAR*)"mypassword", SQL_NTS);
    // Allocate statement handle
    ret = SQLAllocHandle(SQL_HANDLE_STMT, dbc, &stmt);
    // Execute the query
    ret = SQLExecDirect(stmt, (SQLCHAR*)"SELECT thickness FROM substance WHERE texture='Medium' AND thickness='A'", SQL_NTS);
    // Fetch the result
    SQLCHAR thickness[256];
    SQLLEN length;
    ret = SQLFetch(stmt);
    if (ret == SQL_SUCCESS) {
        SQLGetData(stmt, 1, SQL_C_CHAR, thickness, sizeof(thickness), &length);
        std::cout << "The corresponding letter is: " << thickness << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "No result found." << std::endl;
    }
    // Free resources
    SQLFreeHandle(SQL_HANDLE_STMT, stmt);
    SQLDisconnect(dbc);
    SQLFreeHandle(SQL_HANDLE_DBC, dbc);
    SQLFreeHandle(SQL_HANDLE_ENV, env);
    return 0;
}
```
This code connects to the database using a DSN named "mydsn", with the username "myuser" and password "mypassword". You would need to replace these values with the actual credentials for your database. The code then executes the SQL query and fetches the result. If a result is found, it prints the corresponding letter to the console. Finally, it frees the resources used by the ODBC API.

One approach for finding the second largest integer element in an unsorted array is to iterate through the array and keep track of the largest and second largest elements seen so far. Here's a C++ function that implements this approach:
```
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    int largest = INT_MIN;
    int secondLargest = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > largest) {
            secondLargest = largest;
            largest = arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] > secondLargest && arr[i] != largest) {
            secondLargest = arr[i];
        }
    }
    if (secondLargest == INT_MIN) {
        cout << "There is no second largest element." << endl;
        return -1;
    }
    else {
        return secondLargest;
    }
}
```
This function takes an array of integers `arr` and its size `n` as inputs, and returns the second largest integer element in the array. The function first initializes the largest and second largest variables to the minimum integer value. It then iterates through the array, updating the largest and second largest variables as necessary. If there is no second largest element, the function returns -1 and displays a message indicating that there is no second largest element.
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), which is optimal for finding the second largest element in an unsorted array.

