To adjust the theoretical maximum download speed of a cellular network in varying weather conditions, such as rain and snow, we need to take into account the impact of these weather conditions on the signal strength of the network. This can be done by using an empirical formula that takes into account the attenuation of the signal due to the weather condition. The formula can be implemented in C++ as follows:
```
double adjustMaxDownloadSpeed(double maxSpeed, double rainIntensity, double snowIntensity, double windSpeed, double temperature) {
    // Calculate the attenuation due to rain
    double rainAttenuation = 0.0;
    if (rainIntensity > 0.0) {
        rainAttenuation = 0.012 * pow(rainIntensity, 0.625);
    }

    // Calculate the attenuation due to snow
    double snowAttenuation = 0.0;
    if (snowIntensity > 0.0) {
        snowAttenuation = 0.037 * pow(snowIntensity, 0.625);
    }

    // Calculate the attenuation due to wind
    double windAttenuation = 0.0;
    if (windSpeed > 0.0) {
        windAttenuation = 0.05 * pow(windSpeed, 0.3);
    }

    // Calculate the attenuation due to temperature
    double tempAttenuation = 0.0;
    if (temperature < 0.0) {
        tempAttenuation = 0.004 * pow(maxSpeed, 0.5) * (0.1 - temperature);
    }

    // Calculate the total attenuation
    double totalAttenuation = rainAttenuation + snowAttenuation + windAttenuation + tempAttenuation;

    // Adjust the maximum download speed
    double adjustedSpeed = maxSpeed * exp(-totalAttenuation);

    return adjustedSpeed;
}
```
This function takes as input the maximum download speed of the network, as well as the intensity of rain, snow, wind, and temperature. It then calculates the attenuation due to each of these factors and adds them up to get the total attenuation. Finally, it adjusts the maximum download speed by multiplying it by the exponential of the negative total attenuation.
