Yes, an adapter is a design pattern that allows two incompatible interfaces to work together. In the context of displaying data from a JSON file in a table, an adapter can be used to transform the data from the JSON format into a format that can be displayed in the table.
Here is an example of how an adapter can be implemented in JavaScript code:
Let's say you have a JSON file with the following data:
```
{
  "employees": [
    {
      "name": "John Doe",
      "age": 30,
      "position": "Manager"
    },
    {
      "name": "Jane Smith",
      "age": 25,
      "position": "Developer"
    }
  ]
}
```
And you want to display this data in an HTML table with the following structure:
```
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name</th>
      <th>Age</th>
      <th>Position</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <!-- Data rows will go here -->
  </tbody>
</table>
```
To create an adapter that transforms the JSON data into the table format, you can write the following JavaScript code:
```
const json = {
  "employees": [
    {
      "name": "John Doe",
      "age": 30,
      "position": "Manager"
    },
    {
      "name": "Jane Smith",
      "age": 25,
      "position": "Developer"
    }
  ]
};
const tableData = json.employees.map(employee => {
  return {
    Name: employee.name,
    Age: employee.age,
    Position: employee.position
  };
});
const table = document.querySelector('table');
tableData.forEach(data => {
  const row = document.createElement('tr');
  Object.values(data).forEach(value => {
    const cell = document.createElement('td');
    cell.textContent = value;
    row.appendChild(cell);
  });
  table.querySelector('tbody').appendChild(row);
});
```
In this example, we first create a new array called `tableData` by mapping over the `employees` array in the JSON data and transforming each object into a new object with properties that match the column names of the table.
We then use `forEach` to loop over the `tableData` array and create a new row for each object. For each row, we loop over the values of the object and create a new cell for each value, setting the text content of the cell to the value. We then append each cell to the row and each row to the table's `tbody`.

Here is a Java program that sorts the given set of numbers and computes its median:
