One way to approach this problem is to use natural language processing (NLP) techniques to extract relevant features from the text and then use a machine learning algorithm to classify the text into the appropriate category. Here are the steps you can follow:
1. Collect a dataset of statements that are labeled as either "International Relations" or "National Security". You can use existing datasets or create your own by manually labeling statements.
2. Preprocess the text by removing stop words, punctuation, and converting all the text to lowercase.
3. Extract features from the text using techniques such as bag-of-words, n-grams, or word embeddings.
4. Split the dataset into training and testing sets.
5. Train a machine learning algorithm such as Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, or Support Vector Machines (SVM) on the training set.
6. Evaluate the performance of the algorithm on the testing set using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score.
7. Once the algorithm is trained, you can use it to classify new statements into the appropriate category.
Here's a sample code snippet that demonstrates how to train a Naive Bayes classifier on a dataset of statements:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using Accord.MachineLearning;
using Accord.Math.Optimization.Losses;
using Accord.Statistics.Filters;
namespace TextClassification
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load the dataset
            var data = LoadData("data.csv");
            // Preprocess the text
            var preprocessor = new EnglishStemming();
            data = PreprocessData(data, preprocessor);
            // Extract features from the text
            var featureExtractor = new BagOfWords();
            var features = ExtractFeatures(data, featureExtractor);
            // Split the dataset into training and testing sets
            var split = new Split(0.8);
            var splitData = split.Apply(features);
            // Train a Naive Bayes classifier
            var classifier = new NaiveBayesLearning();
            classifier.Options.InnerOption = new MultinomialOptions { LaplaceSmoothing = 1 };
            classifier.Learn(splitData.TrainingInputs, splitData.TrainingOutputs);
            // Evaluate the performance of the classifier
            var evaluator = new ClassificationEvaluator(splitData.TestingInputs, splitData.TestingOutputs);
            var report = evaluator.Test(classifier.Model);
            Console.WriteLine($"Accuracy: {report.Accuracy}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Precision: {report.Precision}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Recall: {report.Recall}");
            Console.WriteLine($"F1-Score: {report.F1Score}");
            // Classify a new statement
            var statement = "In response to the escalating tensions with neighboring countries, the military has proposed an increase in defense spending.";
            statement = preprocessor.Process(statement);
            var input = featureExtractor.Transform(new[] { statement });
            var output = classifier.Decide(input)[0];
            Console.WriteLine($"Classification: {(output == 0 ? "International Relations" : "National Security")}");
        }
        static List<(string Text, int Label)> LoadData(string filePath)
        {
            var data = new List<(string Text, int Label)>();
            using (var reader = new StreamReader(filePath))
            {
                while (!reader.EndOfStream)
                {
                    var line = reader.ReadLine();
                    var values = line.Split(',');
                    data.Add((values[0], int.Parse(values[1])));
                }
            }
            return data;
        }
        static List<(string Text, int Label)> PreprocessData(List<(string Text, int Label)> data, IPreprocessor preprocessor)
        {
            return data.Select(d => (preprocessor.Process(d.Text), d.Label)).ToList();
        }
        static double[][] ExtractFeatures(List<(string Text, int Label)> data, IFeatureExtractor featureExtractor)
        {
            return featureExtractor.Transform(data.Select(d => d.Text).ToArray());
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `LoadData` function reads the dataset from a CSV file where each row contains a statement and a label. The `PreprocessData` function applies the English stemming algorithm to the text to reduce it to its base form. The `ExtractFeatures` function uses the bag-of-words technique to extract features from the text. The `Split` class splits the dataset into training and testing sets. The `NaiveBayesLearning` class trains a Naive Bayes classifier on the training set. The `ClassificationEvaluator` class evaluates the performance of the classifier on the testing set. Finally, the `Decide` method of the `NaiveBayes` class is used to classify a new statement.

In the context of a PHP function, bravery can be defined as the ability of a function to handle unexpected or challenging situations without crashing or producing errors.
Here's an example function in PHP that takes in an array of integers and returns the sum of all positive integers in the array, while also outputting a message if the sum is greater than 100:
```
function sumPositiveIntegers($arr) {
  $sum = 0;
  foreach ($arr as $num) {
    if ($num > 0) {
      $sum += $num;
    }
  }
  if ($sum > 100) {
    echo "The sum of positive integers is greater than 100!";
  }
  return $sum;
}
```
This function first initializes a variable `$sum` to 0, then iterates through each element in the input array `$arr`. If an element is greater than 0, it adds it to the sum. Finally, it checks if the sum is greater than 100 and outputs a message if it is. The function then returns the sum.

